Overlay patch 0035 - the widened backdrop actually COVERS the rows it claims.

CLASS: (b) upstream bug fix worth sending, and the direct follow-up to 0029 and
0032. Not iOS-gated, for the same reason those two are not: it is a rendering
correctness fix and gating it would put the macOS oracle (the spec's ground
truth) permanently out of parity with the phone.

WHAT THE USER SAW ON 0.0.0.12
---------------------------
The new-game opening, Mario's house exterior, 2x SSAA: the top ~105 px band
that 0029 un-letterboxed shows DUPLICATED TEXTURE ROWS that smear as the camera
pans. Indoor scenes (Peach's castle) show nothing wrong.

WHY - AND IT IS 0032'S OWN DOING, HALF-DONE
-------------------------------------------
Overlay 0032 removed the hand-drawn black bars in `gfx_draw_background` by
widening the LOCALS `backgroundMinY` / `backgroundMaxY` to 0..SCREEN_HEIGHT when
`cam_frame_fills_height()`, which makes both `gDPFillWideRectangle` letterbox
fills no-ops. That is right for the fill path - the non-texture backdrop is
drawn from those same two locals, so it grew with them.

The TEXTURE path does not read them. `port_appendGfx_background_texture()`
(`src/port/patches/BackgroundPatches.c`) takes its own copies straight from
`gGameStatusPtr->background{Min,Max}Y` and emits its texrects from `bgMinY` to
`bgMinY + bgMaxY - 1` - native rows 20..219 for PM64's 296x200 backdrops. So
after 0032, on any map WITH a backdrop image (i.e. every outdoor one):

  * rows 0..19 and 220..239 get no colour write at all - not the backdrop, not
    the bgColor fill (which is also clamped), and no longer the black bars;
  * 3D geometry that reaches into the band draws over whatever was in the
    framebuffer;
  * and PM64 captures the framebuffer every frame (`port_emitPrevFrameCapture`,
    `gfx_frame.c:72`) for its shared-framebuffer effects, so the stale rows are
    carried FORWARD - which is the smear, not a static band.

Indoor maps ship no backdrop header, take the `!BACKGROUND_FLAG_TEXTURE` fill
path, and are therefore correct already: exactly what the user observed.

THE FIX, TWO HALVES
-------------------
1. `src/background_gfx.c` - when the camera fills the screen, clear the FULL
   screen to `bgColor` before the backdrop is drawn. The fill colour is already
   set to `camera->bgColor` and the RDP is already in G_CYC_FILL at that point,
   so this is one rectangle. It is the insurance half: after it, no pixel of the
   frame can ever be stale, whichever backdrop path runs (texture, non-texture,
   or the sine-wave strip path, which this patch deliberately leaves alone -
   its strips are per-row and stretching them is a different change).

2. `src/port/patches/BackgroundPatches.c` - when the camera fills the screen,
   the tiled backdrop texrects span 0..SCREEN_HEIGHT-1 instead of
   bgMinY..bgMinY+bgMaxY-1, with the T coordinate biased by -bgMinY rows so the
   image still lands on its native rows. The extra 20 rows top and bottom are
   the image's own EDGE ROW repeated, because the tile is already clamped in T:
   `interpreter.cpp:3822` rewrites `cmt == G_TX_WRAP && maskt == G_TX_NOMASK`
   (which is exactly what `gDPLoadTextureTile` is called with here) to
   `G_TX_CLAMP`. So no tile state changes - only the rectangle grows, and the
   sampler was always going to clamp. Against the oracle's 4:3 frame the top
   band is sky continuing into sky, which is what a widescreen fit should look
   like; the bottom band sits behind the map's terrain in every scene checked.

WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
--------------------------------
* No camera field is written, exactly as after 0029 and 0032.
* Both halves are gated on `cam_frame_fills_height()` - the same predicate 0032
  introduced and 0029's two hunks fire on - so the game's own letterboxed
  cameras (the demo's 177-tall viewport, the title and file select's 184, the
  intro's 162, every `cam_api` cutscene viewport) and 4:3 or narrower are
  untouched and still frame themselves.
* The title screen's `gBackroundNoTileFill` path is untouched: the title camera
  fails `cam_frame_fills_height()`.
* The wave path keeps its native rows; the bgColor clear covers its bands.

WHERE THE HUNKS SIT
-------------------
* `src/background_gfx.c`: one hunk at the top of the backdrop block, ~14 lines
  after overlay 0032's hunk in the same file - no shared context line.
* `src/port/patches/BackgroundPatches.c`: no other patch in the series touches
  this file.

